<h2>The question that reached the Court</h2>
<p>The National Book Trust was brought into existence by Government of India resolutions of 1957 and 1962 to publish and disseminate good literature at moderate prices. In 2012, a Full Bench of the Delhi High Court held that the Trust is an instrumentality of the Government and hence an &ldquo;other authority&rdquo; within Article 12. The Trust appealed, pressing three points: it is a registered society and not a statutory corporation; government grants are only one stream of its income, the rest coming from sale of its publications; and declaring it a State would drag in all the consequences of government employment. A Bench of Justices Prashant Kumar Mishra and Augustine George Masih, assisted by an amicus curiae, rejected each contention and dismissed the appeal.</p>

<h2>The test the Court applied</h2>
<p>The judgment retraces the arc of Article 12 jurisprudence — from the early electricity board and statutory corporation cases through the instrumentality doctrine — and settles on the now-standard cumulative inquiry: is the body financially, functionally and administratively dominated by or under the control of the Government, and is that control particular to the body and pervasive, rather than the general regulatory control to which any society or company is subject? The familiar indicia — government share capital or near-complete financial support, monopoly status, deep and pervasive control, functions of public importance, transfer of a government department — remain indicative, not conclusive. Two propositions frame the analysis at either end: the form of incorporation is irrelevant, and the performance of a public function, by itself, is not enough.</p>

<h2>Why the Trust crossed the line</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Origin</h3><p>The Trust was conceived and structured by the Government itself to pursue a national objective. It was a governmental creation from birth, not a private initiative later brought under regulation.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Administration</h3><p>The Chairman is appointed by the Government and holds office at its pleasure. Ministry representatives sit on the governing body and the Executive Committee, whose regulation-making power is itself subject to government approval.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Finance</h3><p>Financial matters pass through a Financial Adviser, with disagreements escalating to the Ministers. Annual reports and accounts go to the Government, and the Comptroller and Auditor-General audits the books.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Own income</h3><p>Revenue from the sale of publications does not displace the picture. A body pervasively controlled by the Government does not exit Article 12 because it also earns.</p></div>
</div>
<p>On this matrix the Court found the control cumulative and particular: the Government does not merely regulate the Trust as it regulates all societies — it runs the levers that matter.</p>

<h2>The equally important limits</h2>
<p>The judgment is as significant for what it refuses to hold. Being &ldquo;State&rdquo; for Article 12 does not convert the Trust into a government department; it does not make every employee a civil servant; and it does not import the service-law protections of Part XIV of the Constitution. Article 12, the Court emphasised, is a definition for the purposes of Part III — it identifies who must answer for fundamental rights, nothing more. The Court also declined to treat publishing as an exclusively governmental function, reiterating that public function alone, divorced from control, cannot make a body a State. Later developments in the Trust&rsquo;s activities were treated only as corroborative, not as the foundation of the conclusion.</p>

<h2>What the ruling means in practice</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Writ amenability:</strong> employees, authors, vendors and members of the public dealing with the Trust — and with bodies whose control structure resembles it — can invoke Articles 226 and 32 for breach of fundamental rights, including arbitrary State action under Article 14.</li>
  <li><strong>Choice of forum still matters:</strong> a writ court may decline disputed questions of fact or contractual claims; the declaration opens a door, it does not close the civil court&rsquo;s.</li>
  <li><strong>Drafting the challenge:</strong> a petition against a body of contested status should plead the control matrix with documents — the founding resolution, the composition of governing organs, the appointment and removal powers, the funding and audit trail — because that is precisely the material on which these cases turn.</li>
  <li><strong>Service consequences are not automatic:</strong> counsel advising employees of such bodies should not read the ruling as importing government pay, tenure or Article 311 protections; those depend on the body&rsquo;s own rules.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> When maintainability under Article 12 is likely to be disputed, plead the instrumentality case in the petition itself rather than leaving it for rejoinder. Annex the constitutive documents and the latest annual report; the financial, functional and administrative strands should each be separately demonstrated, since the test is cumulative and a petition strong on one strand alone invites dismissal at the threshold.</p>
</div>
